  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-9x-11$ have a local minimum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local minimum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=3{x}^{2}+6x-9.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+6x-9=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=-3,\\,1.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-3$ and $x=1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-4,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(-4) & =3(-4)^{2}+6(-4)-9=15>0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(0) & =3(0)^{2}+6(0)-9=-9<0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(2) & =3(2)^{2}+6(2)-9=15>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-4$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{\u2032}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\u2197$ | max | $\u2198$ | min | $\u2197$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-3,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-3$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x=1.$",
